# THIS REPOSITORY'S OWN COMPOSE DAG. One row per `(parent, child)` pair of templates, with how
# many times the parent splices the child.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ IT SITS BESIDE `rank/graph_edges.sqlc` AND THAT IS THE POINT. That relation is the edge
#    list of the graphs the MODEL composes; this is the edge list of the graph this REPOSITORY
#    composes. Same shape, and `references/substitution.md`'s claim is that they are the same
#    algebra: a term over named closed expressions with substitution as the only operator. Two
#    edge lists in one directory is that claim stopping being a sentence.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND `splices` IS WHERE THE TWO GRAPHS RULE OPPOSITE WAYS ON ONE FACT. A parent composing a
#    child twice is ORDINARY: `references/planner.md` measured it and the repeated relation is
#    read once, shared hit 9,883 and read 0, because a query is IDEMPOTENT. The identical shape
#    in `pm.part` is `checks/jagged_layer`, a VIOLATION, because supply is a CONSERVED carrier
#    and one total closes over both occurrences. ⭐ That difference is what this model adds to
#    `sql-composer` and to BPMN 2.0. `queries/observations/19-duplication.sqlc` is the crossing,
#    and it is a query because both sides are relations.
#
# ⚠️ IT IS FILESYSTEM-FED AND OUTSIDE `pm`, WHICH IS A LINE WORTH KNOWING WHERE IT IS. Everything
#   in `pm` descends from a filed document; this descends from `assets/sqlc/`. So a relation may
#   read it to say something about THIS REPOSITORY and must never join it to a filing to say
#   something about a subject. Same line `diagrams/lane_grain.sqlc` crosses to read
#   `pg_constraint`.
#
# ⭐ THE SCAN IS NOT THE POINT: THE JOIN IS. `examples/shared/tree/mod.rs` is the one scanner, shared by
#   `compositions`, `soundness` and `observations`. What a `BTreeMap` in a Rust program cannot do
#   is meet a relation, so any law that bounds one side of the tree by the other needs the DAG
#   here rather than there.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ AND `inner_joins` IS THE SYNTAX. Who composes whom does not say HOW, and the how is what
#    decides whether a bound survives:
#    driving a query from a relation or left-joining it asks *what is missing from the whole*,
#    inner-joining it asks *does this one pair exist*, and the second hands the whole composed
#    relation to every consumer downstream. It is in the SQL text, so the program that reads the
#    text classifies it and the fact lands here rather than in a second scanner.
#
# ⚠️ AND `assets/dag/edges.sql` CAN GO STALE, which is the cost of the generate-then-load shape.
#   `examples/soundness/main.rs` re-derives the pairs through the same module and compares, so a
#   `:compose` added without rerunning the emitter fails the build rather than being loaded as
#   an older tree. That is a staleness check and NOT two independent routes: it shares the
#   scanner, so it catches the artifact drifting and could not catch the scanner being wrong.
-- public.compose_edge, generated by examples/compositions/main.rs from assets/sqlc/.
SELECT e.parent, e.child, e.splices, e.inner_joins
FROM public.compose_edge e
